  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Provision of Urgent Camp Coordination and Camp Management, Shelter and Health Services in Ma&#x2019;rib Al Wadi and Hays</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The proposed intervention will aim to address immediate needs of IDP, and host community populations severely impacted by recent weather incidents (flooding and violent windstorms) and residing in acute watery diarrhea (AWD)/ cholera hotspots. IOM will implement critical Camp Coordination and Camp Management (CCCM), Shelter and Non-Food Items (SNFI) and Health assistance in Al Hudaydah (Hays district) and Ma&#x2019;rib (Ma&#x2019;rib Alwadi). A total of 36,197 individuals will benefit from these services in target locations.In Hays, to contribute to the broader AWD / cholera response in the district, IOM will support the operation of the established Diarrheal Treatment Center (DTC, previously supported by ICRC) to ensure continued capacity to manage AWD and suspected cholera cases. The DTC services are expected to directly benefit 3,000 IDPs and host community members with AWD treatment, while 2,100 people will benefit from awareness raising.  Further, in recognition of largely unmet needs in informal/ unmanaged settlements in Hays, IOM will provide CCCM and SNFI assistance in two new sites (Al Qulma, and Al Haet). The targeted sites will be supported with site registration, establishment of community management structures and Cash for Shelter/ Transitional Shelter services. A total of 12,005 individuals are expected to benefit from CCCM support in Hays, and 2,898 from shelter support. Overall, a total of 15,005 individuals will benefit from IOM&#x2019;s multisectoral support in Hays.  In Ma&#x2019;rib, IOM will implement CCCM activities in Al Ezz camp, Al Ramsah, An Nour, Asit, Saylat Almil, Ma&#x2019;rib Al Wadi, one of the districts severely impacted by violent windstorms and flooding in August 2024. Activities to be implemented will include essential risk reduction works, such as rehabilitation and/ or construction of risk reduction infrastructure (soil embankments, Gabion walls) as well as awareness raising and deployment of emergency committees to ensure sites have the capacity to mitigate impact of severe weather incidents. In addition, IOM will support target households across displacement sites in Ma&#x2019;rib Al Wadi with transitional shelters, or Cash for Shelter assistance, restoring safe and dignified living conditions that were destroyed in recent weeks. A total of 17,195 individuals will benefit from CCCM services and 3,997 from shelter services in Ma&#x2019;rib Al Wadi. Overall, 21,192 individuals in Ma&#x2019;rib Al Wadi will benefit from activities implemented under this intervention.</narrative>
  </description>
